{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 10, :comments ["<b>And they shall make an ark.</b> The reason that in the case of the ark HaShem changed His word from that of all the other vessels — for concerning all of them He said ״וְעָשִׂיתָ שֻׁלְחָן״, ״וְעָשִׂיתָ מְנוֹרַת״, etc.; ״וְאֶת הַמִּשְׁכָּן תַּעֲשֶׂה״; ״וְעָשִׂיתָ אֶת הַמִּזְבֵּחַ עֲצֵי״, etc. — and even regarding the details of the ark itself He concluded speaking in the second person, as it is written ״וְצִפִּיתָ אוֹתוֹ״, ״וְיָצַקְתָּ לּוֹ״, ״וְעָשִׂיתָ בַדֵּי״, etc. — perhaps He alluded that the essence of the Torah can endure only through the collective of all Israel, and no single existence in the world can perform all the fundamental principles of the Torah. And this is your sign: if he is a priest, then he does not fulfill the giving of the twenty-four priestly gifts and the redemption of the firstborn, etc.; and if he is an Israelite, then he cannot fulfill the positive commandments involved in the offering of sacrifices and their laws, which are numerous positive commandments among them; and likewise a Levite. But through the collective of all Israel they fulfill the totality of the fundamental principles of the Torah. Therefore He said <b>and they shall make</b> in the plural, and concerning the other details of the ark's construction He spoke in the singular — for to prepare the instruments of Torah, which are those who learn it and toil in it and its prerequisites, even a single individual can fulfill." "<b>Further,</b> perhaps He prefaced to speak to him in this language in order to reveal to him regarding the entire undertaking: that although He speaks with him face to face, the commandment did not come to him but to others — namely, they are the wise-hearted (Exodus 36:1), as He explains further on — and He attributes the matter to him because he is the one who commands Israel." "Further, according to what the Sages said (Rashi on Deuteronomy 8:1), that Moses made an ark of wood and placed the tablets there immediately upon his descent from the mountain, until Bezalel made the ark that was set there permanently — and so it says in the portion of Eikev (Deuteronomy 10:1): ״וְעָשִׂיתָ לְּךָ אֲרוֹן עֵץ״ — He was precise in saying ״לְּךָ״, meaning: this ״וְעָשִׂיתָ״ is not like the other ״וְעָשִׂיתָ״ instances that I commanded you concerning the making of the Tabernacle — ״וְעָשִׂיתָ שֻׁלְחָן״, ״וְעָשִׂיתָ מְנוֹרַת״, ״וְעָשִׂיתָ מִזְבֵּחַ״ — none of which were made except by Bezalel. This thing I command you to make, and so he did, as it is written (Deuteronomy 10:3): ״וָאַעַשׂ אֲרוֹן עֲצֵי שִׁטִּים״, and it is written (Deuteronomy 10:5): ״וָאָשִׂים אֶת הַלּוּחוֹת בָּאָרוֹן אֲשֶׁר עָשִׂיתִי״. Therefore He said here <b>and they shall make</b> — meaning, besides what you shall make, which was temporary, those who make it shall also make it from the assets of the Mishkan donation. And if He had said ״וְעָשִׂיתָ״, Moses would have reasoned from what He revealed to him concerning the temporary ark — that since that one was his, this one too would be the same; or he would think that it would be that very same ark that he had already made. Therefore He said: not so — <b>and they shall make</b>, etc. And concerning the other works, where there was no making by anyone other than the aforementioned, Scripture spoke in its usual way, ״וְעָשִׂיתָ״, to say that the merit is directed to Moses even though others perform it."], :book :exodus, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 25, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:33:28.281654687Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}